<body>
<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>

<pre>

<b>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</b>

     Our gain in net worth during 1988 was $569 million, or=20
20.0%.  Over the last 24 years (that is, since present management=20
took over), our per-share book value has grown from $19.46 to=20
$2,974.52, or at a rate of 23.0% compounded annually.

     We=92ve emphasized in past reports that what counts, however,=20
is intrinsic business value - the figure, necessarily an=20
estimate, indicating what all of our constituent businesses are=20
worth.  By our calculations, Berkshire=92s intrinsic business value=20
significantly exceeds its book value.  Over the 24 years,=20
business value has grown somewhat faster than book value; in=20
1988, however, book value grew the faster, by a bit.

     Berkshire=92s past rates of gain in both book value and=20
business value were achieved under circumstances far different=20
from those that now exist.  Anyone ignoring these differences=20
makes the same mistake that a baseball manager would were he to=20
judge the future prospects of a 42-year-old center fielder on the=20
basis of his lifetime batting average.

     Important negatives affecting our prospects today are: (1) a=20
less attractive stock market than generally existed over the past=20
24 years; (2) higher corporate tax rates on most forms of=20
investment income; (3) a far more richly-priced market for the=20
acquisition of businesses; and (4) industry conditions for=20
Capital Cities/ABC, Inc., GEICO Corporation, and The Washington=20
Post Company - Berkshire=92s three permanent investments,=20
constituting about one-half of our net worth - that range from=20
slightly to materially less favorable than those existing five to=20
ten years ago.  All of these companies have superb management and=20
strong properties.  But, at current prices, their upside=20
potential looks considerably less exciting to us today than it=20
did some years ago.

     The major problem we face, however, is a growing capital=20
base.  You=92ve heard that from us before, but this problem, like=20
age, grows in significance each year. (And also, just as with=20
age, it=92s better to have this problem continue to grow rather=20
than to have it =93solved.=94)

     Four years ago I told you that we needed profits of $3.9=20
billion to achieve a 15% annual return over the decade then=20
ahead.  Today, for the next decade, a 15% return demands profits=20
of $10.3 billion.  That seems like a very big number to me and to=20
Charlie Munger, Berkshire=92s Vice Chairman and my partner. (Should=20
that number indeed prove too big, Charlie will find himself, in=20
future reports, retrospectively identified as the senior=20
partner.)

     As a partial offset to the drag that our growing capital=20
base exerts upon returns, we have a very important advantage now=20
that we lacked 24 years ago.  Then, all our capital was tied up=20
in a textile business with inescapably poor economic=20
characteristics.  Today part of our capital is invested in some=20
really exceptional businesses.

     Last year we dubbed these operations the Sainted Seven:=20
Buffalo News, Fechheimer, Kirby, Nebraska Furniture Mart, Scott=20
Fetzer Manufacturing Group, See=92s, and World Book.  In 1988 the=20
Saints came marching in.  You can see just how extraordinary=20
their returns on capital were by examining the historical-cost=20
financial statements on page 45, which combine the figures of the=20
Sainted Seven with those of several smaller units.  With no=20
benefit from financial leverage, this group earned about 67% on=20
average equity capital.

     In most cases the remarkable performance of these units=20
arises partially from an exceptional business franchise; in all=20
cases an exceptional management is a vital factor.  The=20
contribution Charlie and I make is to leave these managers alone.

     In my judgment, these businesses, in aggregate, will=20
continue to produce superb returns.  We=92ll need these: Without=20
this help Berkshire would not have a chance of achieving our 15%=20
goal.  You can be sure that our operating managers will deliver;=20
the question mark in our future is whether Charlie and I can=20
effectively employ the funds that they generate.

     In that respect, we took a step in the right direction early=20
in 1989 when we purchased an 80% interest in Borsheim=92s, a=20
jewelry business in Omaha.  This purchase, described later in=20
this letter, delivers exactly what we look for: an outstanding=20
business run by people we like, admire, and trust.  It=92s a great=20
way to start the year.

<b>Accounting Changes</b>

     We have made a significant accounting change that was=20
mandated for 1988, and likely will have another to make in 1990. =20
When we move figures around from year to year, without any change=20
in economic reality, one of our always-thrilling discussions of=20
accounting is necessary.

     First, I=92ll offer my customary disclaimer: Despite the=20
shortcomings of generally accepted accounting principles (GAAP),=20
I would hate to have the job of devising a better set of rules. =20
The limitations of the existing set, however, need not be=20
inhibiting: CEOs are free to treat GAAP statements as a beginning=20
rather than an end to their obligation to inform owners and=20
creditors - and indeed they should.  After all, any manager of a=20
subsidiary company would find himself in hot water if he reported=20
barebones GAAP numbers that omitted key information needed by his=20
boss, the parent corporation=92s CEO.  Why, then, should the CEO=20
himself withhold information vitally useful to <i>his</i> bosses - the=20
shareholder-owners of the corporation?

     What needs to be reported is data - whether GAAP, non-GAAP,=20
or extra-GAAP - that helps financially-literate readers answer=20
three key questions: (1) Approximately how much is this company=20
worth?  (2) What is the likelihood that it can meet its future=20
obligations? and (3) How good a job are its managers doing, given=20
the hand they have been dealt?

     In most cases, answers to one or more of these questions are=20
somewhere between difficult and impossible to glean from the=20
minimum GAAP presentation.  The business world is simply too=20
complex for a single set of rules to effectively describe=20
economic reality for all enterprises, particularly those=20
operating in a wide variety of businesses, such as Berkshire.

     Further complicating the problem is the fact that many=20
managements view GAAP not as a standard to be met, but as an=20
obstacle to overcome.  Too often their accountants willingly=20
assist them. (=93How much,=94 says the client, =93is two plus two?=94=20
Replies the cooperative accountant, =93What number did you have in=20
mind?=94) Even honest and well-intentioned managements sometimes=20
stretch GAAP a bit in order to present figures they think will=20
more appropriately describe their performance.  Both the=20
smoothing of earnings and the =93big bath=94 quarter are =93white lie=94=20
techniques employed by otherwise upright managements.

     Then there are managers who actively use GAAP to deceive and=20
defraud.  They know that many investors and creditors accept GAAP=20
results as gospel.  So these charlatans interpret the rules=20
=93imaginatively=94 and record business transactions in ways that=20
technically comply with GAAP but actually display an economic=20
illusion to the world.

     As long as investors - including supposedly sophisticated=20
institutions - place fancy valuations on reported =93earnings=94 that=20
march steadily upward, you can be sure that some managers and=20
promoters will exploit GAAP to produce such numbers, no matter=20
what the truth may be.  Over the years, Charlie and I have=20
observed many accounting-based frauds of staggering size.  Few of=20
the perpetrators have been punished; many have not even been=20
censured.  It has been far safer to steal large sums with a pen=20
than small sums with a gun.

     Under one major change mandated by GAAP for 1988, we have=20
been required to fully consolidate all our subsidiaries in our=20
balance sheet and earnings statement.  In the past, Mutual=20
Savings and Loan, and Scott Fetzer Financial (a credit company=20
that primarily finances installment sales of World Book and Kirby=20
products) were consolidated on a =93one-line=94 basis.  That meant we=20
(1) showed our equity in their combined net worths as a single-
entry asset on Berkshire=92s consolidated balance sheet and (2)=20
included our equity in their combined annual earnings as a=20
single-line income entry in our consolidated statement of=20
earnings.  Now the rules require that we consolidate each asset=20
and liability of these companies in our balance sheet and each=20
item of their income and expense in our earnings statement.

     This change underscores the need for companies also to=20
report segmented data: The greater the number of economically=20
diverse business operations lumped together in conventional=20
financial statements, the less useful those presentations are and=20
the less able investors are to answer the three questions posed=20
earlier.  Indeed, the only reason we ever prepare consolidated=20
figures at Berkshire is to meet outside requirements.  On the=20
other hand, Charlie and I constantly study our segment data.

     Now that we are required to bundle more numbers in our GAAP=20
statements, we have decided to publish additional supplementary=20
information that we think will help you measure both business=20
value and managerial performance. (Berkshire=92s ability to=20
discharge its obligations to creditors - the third question we=20
listed - should be obvious, whatever statements you examine.) In=20
these supplementary presentations, we will not necessarily follow=20
GAAP procedures, or even corporate structure.  Rather, we will=20
attempt to lump major business activities in ways that aid=20
analysis but do not swamp you with detail.  Our goal is to give=20
you important information in a form that we would wish to get it=20
if our roles were reversed.

     On pages 41-47 we show separate combined balance sheets and=20
earnings statements for: (1) our subsidiaries engaged in finance-
type operations, which are Mutual Savings and Scott Fetzer=20
Financial; (2) our insurance operations, with their major=20
investment positions itemized; (3) our manufacturing, publishing=20
and retailing businesses, leaving aside certain non-operating=20
assets and purchase-price accounting adjustments; and (4) an all-
other category that includes the non-operating assets (primarily=20
marketable securities) held by the companies in (3) as well as=20
various assets and debts of the Wesco and Berkshire parent=20
companies.

     If you combine the earnings and the net worths of these four=20
segments, you will derive totals matching those shown on our GAAP=20
statements.  However, we want to emphasize that our new=20
presentation does not fall within the purview of our auditors,=20
who in no way bless it. (In fact, they may be horrified; I don=92t=20
want to ask.)

     I referred earlier to a major change in GAAP that is=20
expected in 1990.  This change relates to the calculation of=20
deferred taxes, and is both complicated and controversial - so=20
much so that its imposition, originally scheduled for 1989, was=20
postponed for a year.

     When implemented, the new rule will affect us in various=20
ways.  Most important, we will be required to change the way we=20
calculate our liability for deferred taxes on the unrealized=20
appreciation of stocks held by our insurance companies.

     Right now, our liability is layered.  For the unrealized=20
appreciation that dates back to 1986 and earlier years, $1.2=20
billion, we have booked a 28% tax liability.  For the unrealized=20
appreciation built up since, $600 million, the tax liability has=20
been booked at 34%.  The difference reflects the increase in tax=20
rates that went into effect in 1987.

     It now appears, however, that the new accounting rule will=20
require us to establish the entire liability at 34% in 1990,=20
taking the charge against our earnings.  Assuming no change in=20
tax rates by 1990, this step will reduce our earnings in that=20
year (and thereby our reported net worth) by $71 million.  The=20
proposed rule will also affect other items on our balance sheet,=20
but these changes will have only a minor impact on earnings and=20
net worth.

     We have no strong views about the desirability of this=20
change in calculation of deferred taxes.  We should point out,=20
however, that neither a 28% nor a 34% tax liability precisely=20
depicts economic reality at Berkshire since we have no plans to=20
sell the stocks in which we have the great bulk of our gains.

     To those of you who are uninterested in accounting, I=20
apologize for this dissertation.  I realize that many of you do=20
not pore over our figures, but instead hold Berkshire primarily=20
because you know that: (1) Charlie and I have the bulk of our=20
money in Berkshire; (2) we intend to run things so that your=20
gains or losses are in direct proportion to ours; and (3) the=20
record has so far been satisfactory.  There is nothing=20
necessarily wrong with this kind of =93faith=94 approach to=20
investing.  Other shareholders, however, prefer an =93analysis=94=20
approach and we want to supply the information they need.  In our=20
own investing, we search for situations in which both approaches=20
give us the same answer.

<b>Sources of Reported Earnings</b>

     In addition to supplying you with our new four-sector=20
accounting material, we will continue to list the major sources=20
of Berkshire=92s reported earnings just as we have in the past.

     In the following table, amortization of Goodwill and other=20
major purchase-price accounting adjustments are not charged=20
against the specific businesses to which they apply but are=20
instead aggregated and shown separately.  This procedure lets you=20
view the earnings of our businesses as they would have been=20
reported had we not purchased them.  I=92ve explained in past=20
reports why this form of presentation seems to us to be more=20
useful to investors and managers than the standard GAAP=20
presentation, which makes purchase-price adjustments on a=20
business-by-business basis.  The total net earnings we show in=20
the table are, of course, identical to the GAAP total in our=20
audited financial statements.

     Further information about these businesses is given in the=20
Business Segment section on pages 32-34, and in the Management=92s=20
Discussion section on pages 36-40.  In these sections you also=20
will find our segment earnings reported on a GAAP basis.  For=20
information on Wesco=92s businesses, I urge you to read Charlie=20
Munger=92s letter, which starts on page 52.  It contains the best=20
description I have seen of the events that produced the present=20
savings-and-loan crisis.  Also, take special note of Dave=20
Hillstrom=92s performance at Precision Steel Warehouse, a Wesco=20
subsidiary.  Precision operates in an extremely competitive=20
industry, yet Dave consistently achieves good returns on invested=20
capital.  Though data is lacking to prove the point, I think it=20
is likely that his performance, both in 1988 and years past,=20
would rank him number one among his peers.
