     The earnings achieved by our operating businesses are=20
superb, whether measured on an absolute basis or against those of=20
their competitors.  For that we thank our operating managers: You=20
and I are fortunate to be associated with them.

     At Berkshire, associations like these last a long time.  We=20
do not remove superstars from our lineup merely because they have=20
attained a specified age - whether the traditional 65, or the 95=20
reached by Mrs. B on the eve of Hanukkah in 1988.  Superb=20
managers are too scarce a resource to be discarded simply because=20
a cake gets crowded with candles.  Moreover, our experience with=20
newly-minted MBAs has not been that great.  Their academic=20
records always look terrific and the candidates always know just=20
what to say; but too often they are short on personal commitment=20
to the company and general business savvy.  It=92s difficult to=20
teach a new dog old tricks.

     Here=92s an update on our major non-insurance operations:

   <b>o</b> At Nebraska Furniture Mart, Mrs. B (Rose Blumkin) and her=20
cart roll on and on.  She=92s been the boss for 51 years, having=20
started the business at 44 with $500. (Think what she would have=20
done with $1,000!) With Mrs. B, old age will always be ten years=20
away.

     The Mart, long the largest home furnishings store in the=20
country, continues to grow.  In the fall, the store opened a=20
detached 20,000 square foot Clearance Center, which expands our=20
ability to offer bargains in all price ranges.

     Recently Dillard=92s, one of the most successful department=20
store operations in the country, entered the Omaha market.  In=20
many of its stores, Dillard=92s runs a full furniture department,=20
undoubtedly doing well in this line.  Shortly before opening in=20
Omaha, however, William Dillard, chairman of the company,=20
announced that his new store would not sell furniture.  Said he,=20
referring to NFM: =93We don=92t want to compete with them.  We think=20
they are about the best there is.=94

     At the Buffalo News we extol the value of advertising, and=20
our policies at NFM prove that we practice what we preach.  Over=20
the past three years NFM has been the largest ROP advertiser in=20
the Omaha World-Herald. (ROP advertising is the kind printed in=20
the paper, as contrasted to the preprinted-insert kind.) In no=20
other major market, to my knowledge, is a home furnishings=20
operation the leading customer of the newspaper.  At times, we=20
also run large ads in papers as far away as Des Moines, Sioux=20
City and Kansas City - always with good results.  It truly does=20
pay to advertise, as long as you have something worthwhile to=20
offer.

     Mrs. B=92s son, Louie, and his boys, Ron and Irv, complete the=20
winning Blumkin team.  It=92s a joy to work with this family.  All=20
its members have character that matches their extraordinary=20
abilities.

   <b>o</b> Last year I stated unequivocally that pre-tax margins at=20
The Buffalo News would fall in 1988.  That forecast would have=20
proved correct at almost any other newspaper our size or larger. =20
But Stan Lipsey - bless him - has managed to make me look=20
foolish.

     Though we increased our prices a bit less than the industry=20
average last year, and though our newsprint costs and wage rates=20
rose in line with industry norms, Stan actually improved margins=20
a tad.  No one in the newspaper business has a better managerial=20
record.  He has achieved it, furthermore, while running a paper=20
that gives readers an extraordinary amount of news.  We believe=20
that our =93newshole=94 percentage - the portion of the paper devoted=20
to news - is bigger than that of any other dominant paper of our=20
size or larger.  The percentage was 49.5% in 1988 versus 49.8% in=20
1987.  We are committed to keeping it around 50%, whatever the=20
level or trend of profit margins.

     Charlie and I have loved the newspaper business since we=20
were youngsters, and we have had great fun with the News in the=20
12 years since we purchased it.  We were fortunate to find Murray=20
Light, a top-flight editor, on the scene when we arrived and he=20
has made us proud of the paper ever since.

   <b>o</b> See=92s Candies sold a record 25.1 million pounds in 1988. =20
Prospects did not look good at the end of October, but excellent=20
Christmas volume, considerably better than the record set in=20
1987, turned the tide.

     As we=92ve told you before, See=92s business continues to become=20
more Christmas-concentrated.  In 1988, the Company earned a=20
record 90% of its full-year profits in December: $29 million out=20
of $32.5 million before tax. (It=92s enough to make you believe in=20
Santa Claus.) December=92s deluge of business produces a modest=20
seasonal bulge in Berkshire=92s corporate earnings.  Another small=20
bulge occurs in the first quarter, when most World Book annuals=20
are sold.

     Charlie and I put Chuck Huggins in charge of See=92s about=20
five minutes after we bought the company.  Upon reviewing his=20
record, you may wonder what took us so long.

   <b>o</b> At Fechheimer, the Heldmans - Bob, George, Gary, Roger and=20
Fred - are the Cincinnati counterparts of the Blumkins.  Neither=20
furniture retailing nor uniform manufacturing has inherently=20
attractive economics.  In these businesses, only exceptional=20
managements can deliver high returns on invested capital.  And=20
that=92s exactly what the five Heldmans do. (As Mets announcer=20
Ralph Kiner once said when comparing pitcher Steve Trout to his=20
father, Dizzy Trout, the famous Detroit Tigers pitcher: =93There=92s=20
a lot of heredity in that family.=94)

     Fechheimer made a fairly good-sized acquisition in 1988. =20
Charlie and I have such confidence in the business savvy of the=20
Heldman family that we okayed the deal without even looking at=20
it.  There are very few managements anywhere - including those=20
running the top tier companies of the <u>Fortune 500</u> - in which we=20
would exhibit similar confidence.

     Because of both this acquisition and some internal growth,=20
sales at Fechheimer should be up significantly in 1989.

   <b>o</b> All of the operations managed by Ralph Schey - World Book,=20
Kirby, and The Scott Fetzer Manufacturing Group - performed=20
splendidly in 1988.  Returns on the capital entrusted to Ralph=20
continue to be exceptional.

     Within the Scott Fetzer Manufacturing Group, particularly=20
fine progress was recorded at its largest unit, Campbell=20
Hausfeld.  This company, the country=92s leading producer of small=20
and medium-sized air compressors, has more than doubled earnings=20
since 1986.

     Unit sales at both Kirby and World Book were up=20
significantly in 1988, with export business particularly strong. =20
World Book became available in the Soviet Union in September,=20
when that country=92s largest American book store opened in Moscow. =20
Ours is the only general encyclopedia offered at the store.

     Ralph=92s personal productivity is amazing: In addition to=20
running 19 businesses in superb fashion, he is active at The=20
Cleveland Clinic, Ohio University, Case Western Reserve, and a=20
venture capital operation that has spawned sixteen Ohio-based=20
companies and resurrected many others.  Both Ohio and Berkshire=20
are fortunate to have Ralph on their side.

<b>Borsheim=92s</b>

     It was in 1983 that Berkshire purchased an 80% interest in=20
The Nebraska Furniture Mart.  Your Chairman blundered then by=20
neglecting to ask Mrs. B a question any schoolboy would have=20
thought of: =93Are there any more at home like you?=94 Last month I=20
corrected the error: We are now 80% partners with another branch=20
of the family.

     After Mrs. B came over from Russia in 1917, her parents and=20
five siblings followed. (Her two other siblings had preceded=20
her.) Among the sisters was Rebecca Friedman who, with her=20
husband, Louis, escaped in 1922 to the west through Latvia in a=20
journey as perilous as Mrs. B=92s earlier odyssey to the east=20
through Manchuria.  When the family members reunited in Omaha=20
they had no tangible assets.  However, they came equipped with an=20
extraordinary combination of brains, integrity, and enthusiasm=20
for work - and that=92s all they needed.  They have since proved=20
themselves invincible.

     In 1948 Mr. Friedman purchased Borsheim=92s, a small Omaha=20
jewelry store.  He was joined in the business by his son, Ike, in=20
1950 and, as the years went by, Ike=92s son, Alan, and his sons-in-
law, Marvin Cohn and Donald Yale, came in also.

     You won=92t be surprised to learn that this family brings to=20
the jewelry business precisely the same approach that the=20
Blumkins bring to the furniture business.  The cornerstone for=20
both enterprises is Mrs. B=92s creed: =93Sell cheap and tell the=20
truth.=94 Other fundamentals at both businesses are: (1) single=20
store operations featuring huge inventories that provide=20
customers with an enormous selection across all price ranges, (2)=20
daily attention to detail by top management, (3) rapid turnover,=20
(4) shrewd buying, and (5) incredibly low expenses.  The=20
combination of the last three factors lets both stores offer=20
everyday prices that no one in the country comes close to=20
matching.

     Most people, no matter how sophisticated they are in other=20
matters, feel like babes in the woods when purchasing jewelry. =20
They can judge neither quality nor price.  For them only one rule=20
makes sense: If you don=92t know jewelry, know the jeweler.

     I can assure you that those who put their trust in Ike=20
Friedman and his family will never be disappointed.  The way in=20
which we purchased our interest in their business is the ultimate=20
testimonial.  Borsheim=92s had no audited financial statements;=20
nevertheless, we didn=92t take inventory, verify receivables or=20
audit the operation in any way.  Ike simply told us what was so -
- and on that basis we drew up a one-page contract and wrote a=20
large check.

     Business at Borsheim=92s has mushroomed in recent years as the=20
reputation of the Friedman family has spread.  Customers now come=20
to the store from all over the country.  Among them have been=20
some friends of mine from both coasts who thanked me later for=20
getting them there.

