     The property-casualty insurance industry is not only=20
subnormally profitable, it is subnormally popular. (As Sam=20
Goldwyn philosophized: =93In life, one must learn to take the=20
bitter with the sour.=94) One of the ironies of business is that=20
many relatively-unprofitable industries that are plagued by=20
inadequate prices habitually find themselves beat upon by irate=20
customers even while other, hugely profitable industries are=20
spared complaints, no matter how high their prices. =20

     Take the breakfast cereal industry, whose return on invested=20
capital is more than double that of the auto insurance industry=20
(which is why companies like Kellogg and General Mills sell at=20
five times book value and most large insurers sell close to=20
book).  The cereal companies regularly impose price increases,=20
few of them related to a significant jump in their costs.  Yet=20
not a peep is heard from consumers.  But when auto insurers raise=20
prices by amounts that do not even match cost increases,=20
customers are outraged.  If you want to be loved, it=92s clearly=20
better to sell high-priced corn flakes than low-priced auto=20
insurance.

     The antagonism that the public feels toward the industry can=20
have serious consequences: Proposition 103, a California=20
initiative passed last fall, threatens to push auto insurance=20
prices down sharply, even though costs have been soaring.  The=20
price cut has been suspended while the courts review the=20
initiative, but the resentment that brought on the vote has not=20
been suspended: Even if the initiative is overturned, insurers=20
are likely to find it tough to operate profitably in California.=20
(Thank heavens the citizenry isn=92t mad at bonbons: If Proposition=20
103 applied to candy as well as insurance, See=92s would be forced=20
to sell its product for $5.76 per pound. rather than the $7.60 we=20
charge - and would be losing money by the bucketful.)

     The immediate direct effects on Berkshire from the=20
initiative are minor, since we saw few opportunities for profit=20
in the rate structure that existed in California prior to the=20
vote.  However, the forcing down of prices would seriously affect=20
GEICO, our 44%-owned investee, which gets about 10% of its=20
premium volume from California.  Even more threatening to GEICO=20
is the possibility that similar pricing actions will be taken in=20
other states, through either initiatives or legislation.

     If voters insist that auto insurance be priced below cost,=20
it eventually must be sold by government.  Stockholders can=20
subsidize policyholders for a short period, but only taxpayers=20
can subsidize them over the long term.  At most property-casualty=20
companies, socialized auto insurance would be no disaster for=20
shareholders.  Because of the commodity characteristics of the=20
industry, most insurers earn mediocre returns and therefore have=20
little or no economic goodwill to lose if they are forced by=20
government to leave the auto insurance business.  But GEICO,=20
because it is a low-cost producer able to earn high returns on=20
equity, has a huge amount of economic goodwill at risk.  In turn,=20
so do we.

