     Our permanent holdings - Capital Cities/ABC, Inc., GEICO=20
Corporation, and The Washington Post Company - remain unchanged. =20
Also unchanged is our unqualified admiration of their=20
managements: Tom Murphy and Dan Burke at Cap Cities, Bill Snyder=20
and Lou Simpson at GEICO, and Kay Graham and Dick Simmons at The=20
Washington Post.  Charlie and I appreciate enormously the talent=20
and integrity these managers bring to their businesses.

     Their performance, which we have observed at close range,=20
contrasts vividly with that of many CEOs, which we have=20
fortunately observed from a safe distance.  Sometimes these CEOs=20
clearly do not belong in their jobs; their positions,=20
nevertheless, are usually secure.  The supreme irony of business=20
management is that it is far easier for an inadequate CEO to keep=20
his job than it is for an inadequate subordinate.

     If a secretary, say, is hired for a job that requires typing=20
ability of at least 80 words a minute and turns out to be capable=20
of only 50 words a minute, she will lose her job in no time. =20
There is a logical standard for this job; performance is easily=20
measured; and if you can=92t make the grade, you=92re out. =20
Similarly, if new sales people fail to generate sufficient=20
business quickly enough, they will be let go.  Excuses will not=20
be accepted as a substitute for orders.

     However, a CEO who doesn=92t perform is frequently carried=20
indefinitely.  One reason is that performance standards for his=20
job seldom exist.  When they do, they are often fuzzy or they may=20
be waived or explained away, even when the performance shortfalls=20
are major and repeated.  At too many companies, the boss shoots=20
the arrow of managerial performance and then hastily paints the=20
bullseye around the spot where it lands.

     Another important, but seldom recognized, distinction=20
between the boss and the foot soldier is that the CEO has no=20
immediate superior whose performance is itself getting measured. =20
The sales manager who retains a bunch of lemons in his sales=20
force will soon be in hot water himself.  It is in his immediate=20
self-interest to promptly weed out his hiring mistakes. =20
Otherwise, he himself may be weeded out.  An office manager who=20
has hired inept secretaries faces the same imperative.

     But the CEO=92s boss is a Board of Directors that seldom=20
measures itself and is infrequently held to account for=20
substandard corporate performance.  If the Board makes a mistake=20
in hiring, and perpetuates that mistake, so what?  Even if the=20
company is taken over because of the mistake, the deal will=20
probably bestow substantial benefits on the outgoing Board=20
members. (The bigger they are, the softer they fall.)

     Finally, relations between the Board and the CEO are=20
expected to be congenial.  At board meetings, criticism of the=20
CEO=92s performance is often viewed as the social equivalent of=20
belching.  No such inhibitions restrain the office manager from=20
critically evaluating the substandard typist.

     These points should not be interpreted as a blanket=20
condemnation of CEOs or Boards of Directors: Most are able and=20
hard-working, and a number are truly outstanding.  But the=20
management failings that Charlie and I have seen make us thankful=20
that we are linked with the managers of our three permanent=20
holdings.  They love their businesses, they think like owners,=20
and they exude integrity and ability.

