<b>Arbitrage</b>

     In past reports we have told you that our insurance=20
subsidiaries sometimes engage in arbitrage as an alternative to=20
holding short-term cash equivalents. We prefer, of course, to=20
make major long-term commitments, but we often have more cash=20
than good ideas.  At such times, arbitrage sometimes promises=20
much greater returns than Treasury Bills and, equally important,=20
cools any temptation we may have to relax our standards for long-
term investments.  (Charlie=92s sign off after we=92ve talked about=20
an arbitrage commitment is usually: =93Okay, at least it will keep=20
you out of bars.=94)

     During 1988 we made unusually large profits from arbitrage,=20
measured both by absolute dollars and rate of return.  Our pre-
tax gain was about $78 million on average invested funds of about=20
$147 million.

     This level of activity makes some detailed discussion of=20
arbitrage and our approach to it appropriate.  Once, the word=20
applied only to the simultaneous purchase and sale of securities=20
or foreign exchange in two different markets.  The goal was to=20
exploit tiny price differentials that might exist between, say,=20
Royal Dutch stock trading in guilders in Amsterdam, pounds in=20
London, and dollars in New York.  Some people might call this=20
scalping; it won=92t surprise you that practitioners opted for the=20
French term, arbitrage.

     Since World War I the definition of arbitrage - or =93risk=20
arbitrage,=94 as it is now sometimes called - has expanded to=20
include the pursuit of profits from an announced corporate event=20
such as sale of the company, merger, recapitalization,=20
reorganization, liquidation, self-tender, etc.  In most cases the=20
arbitrageur expects to profit regardless of the behavior of the=20
stock market.  The major risk he usually faces instead is that=20
the announced event won=92t happen. =20

     Some offbeat opportunities occasionally arise in the=20
arbitrage field.  I participated in one of these when I was 24=20
and working in New York for Graham-Newman Corp. Rockwood &amp; Co.,=20
a Brooklyn based chocolate products company of limited=20
profitability, had adopted LIFO inventory valuation in 1941=20
when cocoa was selling for 5=A2 per pound.  In 1954 a=20
temporary shortage of cocoa caused the price to soar to over=20
60=A2.  Consequently Rockwood wished to unload its valuable=20
inventory - quickly, before the price dropped.  But if the cocoa=20
had simply been sold off, the company would have owed close to=20
a 50% tax on the proceeds.

     The 1954 Tax Code came to the rescue.  It contained an=20
arcane provision that eliminated the tax otherwise due on LIFO=20
profits if inventory was distributed to shareholders as part of a=20
plan reducing the scope of a corporation=92s business.  Rockwood=20
decided to terminate one of its businesses, the sale of cocoa=20
butter, and said 13 million pounds of its cocoa bean inventory=20
was attributable to that activity.  Accordingly, the company=20
offered to repurchase its stock in exchange for the cocoa beans=20
it no longer needed, paying 80 pounds of beans for each share. =20

     For several weeks I busily bought shares, sold beans, and=20
made periodic stops at Schroeder Trust to exchange stock=20
certificates for warehouse receipts.  The profits were good and=20
my only expense was subway tokens.

     The architect of Rockwood=92s restructuring was an unknown,=20
but brilliant Chicagoan, Jay Pritzker, then 32.  If you=92re=20
familiar with Jay=92s subsequent record, you won=92t be surprised to=20
hear the action worked out rather well for Rockwood=92s continuing=20
shareholders also.  From shortly before the tender until shortly=20
after it, Rockwood stock appreciated from 15 to 100, even though=20
the company was experiencing large operating losses.  Sometimes=20
there is more to stock valuation than price-earnings ratios.

     In recent years, most arbitrage operations have involved=20
takeovers, friendly and unfriendly.  With acquisition fever=20
rampant, with anti-trust challenges almost non-existent, and with=20
bids often ratcheting upward, arbitrageurs have prospered=20
mightily.  They have not needed special talents to do well; the=20
trick, a la Peter Sellers in the movie, has simply been =93Being=20
There.=94 In Wall Street the old proverb has been reworded: =93Give a=20
man a fish and you feed him for a day.  Teach him how to=20
arbitrage and you feed him forever.=94 (If, however, he studied at=20
the Ivan Boesky School of Arbitrage, it may be a state=20
institution that supplies his meals.)

     To evaluate arbitrage situations you must answer four=20
questions: (1) How likely is it that the promised event will=20
indeed occur? (2) How long will your money be tied up? (3) What=20
chance is there that something still better will transpire - a=20
competing takeover bid, for example? and (4) What will happen if=20
the event does not take place because of anti-trust action,=20
financing glitches, etc.?

     Arcata Corp., one of our more serendipitous arbitrage=20
experiences, illustrates the twists and turns of the business. =20
On September 28, 1981 the directors of Arcata agreed in principle=20
to sell the company to Kohlberg, Kravis, Roberts &amp; Co. (KKR),=20
then and now a major leveraged-buy out firm.  Arcata was in the=20
printing and forest products businesses and had one other thing=20
going for it: In 1978 the U.S. Government had taken title to=20
10,700 acres of Arcata timber, primarily old-growth redwood, to=20
expand Redwood National Park.  The government had paid $97.9=20
million, in several installments, for this acreage, a sum Arcata=20
was contesting as grossly inadequate.  The parties also disputed=20
the interest rate that should apply to the period between the=20
taking of the property and final payment for it.  The enabling=20
legislation stipulated 6% simple interest; Arcata argued for a=20
much higher and compounded rate.

     Buying a company with a highly-speculative, large-sized=20
claim in litigation creates a negotiating problem, whether the=20
claim is on behalf of or against the company.  To solve this=20
problem, KKR offered $37.00 per Arcata share plus two-thirds of=20
any additional amounts paid by the government for the redwood=20
lands.

     Appraising this arbitrage opportunity, we had to ask=20
ourselves whether KKR would consummate the transaction since,=20
among other things, its offer was contingent upon its obtaining=20
=93satisfactory financing.=94 A clause of this kind is always=20
dangerous for the seller: It offers an easy exit for a suitor=20
whose ardor fades between proposal and marriage.  However, we=20
were not particularly worried about this possibility because=20
KKR=92s past record for closing had been good.

     We also had to ask ourselves what would happen if the KKR=20
deal did fall through, and here we also felt reasonably=20
comfortable: Arcata=92s management and directors had been shopping=20
the company for some time and were clearly determined to sell. =20
If KKR went away, Arcata would likely find another buyer, though=20
of course, the price might be lower.

     Finally, we had to ask ourselves what the redwood claim=20
might be worth.  Your Chairman, who can=92t tell an elm from an=20
oak, had no trouble with that one: He coolly evaluated the claim=20
at somewhere between zero and a whole lot.

     We started buying Arcata stock, then around $33.50, on=20
September 30 and in eight weeks purchased about 400,000 shares,=20
or 5% of the company.  The initial announcement said that the=20
$37.00 would be paid in January, 1982.  Therefore, if everything=20
had gone perfectly, we would have achieved an annual rate of=20
return of about 40% - not counting the redwood claim, which would=20
have been frosting.

     All did not go perfectly.  In December it was announced that=20
the closing would be delayed a bit.  Nevertheless, a definitive=20
agreement was signed on January 4. Encouraged, we raised our=20
stake, buying at around $38.00 per share and increasing our=20
holdings to 655,000 shares, or over 7% of the company.  Our=20
willingness to pay up - even though the closing had been=20
postponed - reflected our leaning toward =93a whole lot=94 rather=20
than =93zero=94 for the redwoods.

     Then, on February 25 the lenders said they were taking a=20
=93second look=94 at financing terms =93 in view of the severely=20
depressed housing industry and its impact on Arcata=92s outlook.=94=20
The stockholders=92 meeting was postponed again, to April.  An=20
Arcata spokesman said he =93did not think the fate of the=20
acquisition itself was imperiled.=94 When arbitrageurs hear such=20
reassurances, their minds flash to the old saying: =93He lied like=20
a finance minister on the eve of devaluation.=94

     On March 12 KKR said its earlier deal wouldn=92t work, first=20
cutting its offer to $33.50, then two days later raising it to=20
$35.00. On March 15, however, the directors turned this bid down=20
and accepted another group=92s offer of $37.50 plus one-half of any=20
redwood recovery.  The shareholders okayed the deal, and the=20
$37.50 was paid on June 4.

     We received $24.6 million versus our cost of $22.9 million;=20
our average holding period was close to six months.  Considering=20
the trouble this transaction encountered, our 15% annual rate of=20
return excluding any value for the redwood claim - was more than=20
satisfactory.

     But the best was yet to come.  The trial judge appointed two=20
commissions, one to look at the timber=92s value, the other to=20
consider the interest rate questions.  In January 1987, the first=20
commission said the redwoods were worth $275.7 million and the=20
second commission recommended a compounded, blended rate of=20
return working out to about 14%.

     In August 1987 the judge upheld these conclusions, which=20
meant a net amount of about $600 million would be due Arcata. =20
The government then appealed.  In 1988, though, before this=20
appeal was heard, the claim was settled for $519 million. =20
Consequently, we received an additional $29.48 per share, or=20
about $19.3 million.  We will get another $800,000 or so in 1989.

     Berkshire=92s arbitrage activities differ from those of many=20
arbitrageurs.  First, we participate in only a few, and usually=20
very large, transactions each year.  Most practitioners buy into=20
a great many deals perhaps 50 or more per year.  With that many=20
irons in the fire, they must spend most of their time monitoring=20
both the progress of deals and the market movements of the=20
related stocks.  This is not how Charlie nor I wish to spend our=20
lives. (What=92s the sense in getting rich just to stare at a=20
ticker tape all day?)

     Because we diversify so little, one particularly profitable=20
or unprofitable transaction will affect our yearly result from=20
arbitrage far more than it will the typical arbitrage operation. =20
So far, Berkshire has not had a really bad experience.  But we=20
will - and when it happens we=92ll report the gory details to you.

     The other way we differ from some arbitrage operations is=20
that we participate only in transactions that have been publicly=20
announced.  We do not trade on rumors or try to guess takeover=20
candidates.  We just read the newspapers, think about a few of=20
the big propositions, and go by our own sense of probabilities.

     At yearend, our only major arbitrage position was 3,342,000=20
shares of RJR Nabisco with a cost of $281.8 million and a market=20
value of $304.5 million.  In January we increased our holdings to=20
roughly four million shares and in February we eliminated our=20
position.  About three million shares were accepted when we=20
tendered our holdings to KKR, which acquired RJR, and the=20
returned shares were promptly sold in the market.  Our pre-tax=20
profit was a better-than-expected $64 million.

     Earlier, another familiar face turned up in the RJR bidding=20
contest: Jay Pritzker, who was part of a First Boston group that=20
made a tax-oriented offer.  To quote Yogi Berra; =93It was deja vu=20
all over again.=94

     During most of the time when we normally would have been=20
purchasers of RJR, our activities in the stock were restricted=20
because of Salomon=92s participation in a bidding group. =20
Customarily, Charlie and I, though we are directors of Salomon,=20
are walled off from information about its merger and acquisition=20
work.  We have asked that it be that way: The information would=20
do us no good and could, in fact, occasionally inhibit=20
Berkshire=92s arbitrage operations.

     However, the unusually large commitment that Salomon=20
proposed to make in the RJR deal required that all directors be=20
fully informed and involved.  Therefore, Berkshire=92s purchases of=20
RJR were made at only two times: first, in the few days=20
immediately following management=92s announcement of buyout plans,=20
before Salomon became involved; and considerably later, after the=20
RJR board made its decision in favor of KKR.  Because we could=20
not buy at other times, our directorships cost Berkshire=20
significant money.

     Considering Berkshire=92s good results in 1988, you might=20
expect us to pile into arbitrage during 1989.  Instead, we expect=20
to be on the sidelines.

     One pleasant reason is that our cash holdings are down -=20
because our position in equities that we expect to hold for a=20
very long time is substantially up.  As regular readers of this=20
report know, our new commitments are not based on a judgment=20
about short-term prospects for the stock market.  Rather, they=20
reflect an opinion about long-term business prospects for=20
specific companies.  We do not have, never have had, and never=20
will have an opinion about where the stock market, interest=20
rates, or business activity will be a year from now.

     Even if we had a lot of cash we probably would do little in=20
arbitrage in 1989.  Some extraordinary excesses have developed in=20
the takeover field.  As Dorothy says: =93Toto, I have a feeling=20
we=92re not in Kansas any more.=94

     We have no idea how long the excesses will last, nor do we=20
know what will change the attitudes of government, lender and=20
buyer that fuel them.  But we do know that the less the prudence=20
with which others conduct their affairs, the greater the prudence=20
with which we should conduct our own affairs.  We have no desire=20
to arbitrage transactions that reflect the unbridled - and, in=20
our view, often unwarranted - optimism of both buyers and=20
lenders.  In our activities, we will heed the wisdom of Herb=20
Stein: =93If something can=92t go on forever, it will end.=94

<b>Efficient Market Theory</b>

     The preceding discussion about arbitrage makes a small=20
discussion of =93efficient market theory=94 (EMT) also seem relevant. =20
This doctrine became highly fashionable - indeed, almost holy=20
scripture in academic circles during the 1970s.  Essentially, it=20
said that analyzing stocks was useless because all public=20
information about them was appropriately reflected in their=20
prices.  In other words, the market always knew everything.  As a=20
corollary, the professors who taught EMT said that someone=20
throwing darts at the stock tables could select a stock portfolio=20
having prospects just as good as one selected by the brightest,=20
most hard-working security analyst.  Amazingly, EMT was embraced=20
not only by academics, but by many investment professionals and=20
corporate managers as well.  Observing correctly that the market=20
was <i>frequently</i> efficient, they went on to conclude incorrectly=20
that it was <i>always</i> efficient.  The difference between these=20
propositions is night and day.

     In my opinion, the continuous 63-year arbitrage experience=20
of Graham-Newman Corp. Buffett Partnership, and Berkshire=20
illustrates just how foolish EMT is. (There=92s plenty of other=20
evidence, also.) While at Graham-Newman, I made a study of its=20
earnings from arbitrage during the entire 1926-1956 lifespan of=20
the company.  Unleveraged returns averaged 20% per year. =20
Starting in 1956, I applied Ben Graham=92s arbitrage principles,=20
first at Buffett Partnership and then Berkshire.  Though I=92ve not=20
made an exact calculation, I have done enough work to know that=20
the 1956-1988 returns averaged well over 20%. (Of course, I=20
operated in an environment far more favorable than Ben=92s; he had=20
1929-1932 to contend with.)

     All of the conditions are present that are required for a=20
fair test of portfolio performance: (1) the three organizations=20
traded hundreds of different securities while building this 63-
year record; (2) the results are not skewed by a few fortunate=20
experiences; (3) we did not have to dig for obscure facts or=20
develop keen insights about products or managements - we simply=20
acted on highly-publicized events; and (4) our arbitrage=20
positions were a clearly identified universe - they have not been=20
selected by hindsight.

     Over the 63 years, the general market delivered just under a=20
10% annual return, including dividends.  That means $1,000 would=20
have grown to $405,000 if all income had been reinvested.  A 20%=20
rate of return, however, would have produced $97 million.  That=20
strikes us as a statistically-significant differential that=20
might, conceivably, arouse one=92s curiosity.

     Yet proponents of the theory have never seemed interested in=20
discordant evidence of this type.  True, they don=92t talk quite as=20
much about their theory today as they used to.  But no one, to my=20
knowledge, has ever said he was wrong, no matter how many=20
thousands of students he has sent forth misinstructed.  EMT,=20
moreover, continues to be an integral part of the investment=20
curriculum at major business schools.  Apparently, a reluctance=20
to recant, and thereby to demystify the priesthood, is not=20
limited to theologians.

     Naturally the disservice done students and gullible=20
investment professionals who have swallowed EMT has been an=20
extraordinary service to us and other followers of Graham.  In=20
any sort of a contest - financial, mental, or physical - it=92s an=20
enormous advantage to have opponents who have been taught that=20
it=92s useless to even try.  From a selfish point of view,=20
Grahamites should probably endow chairs to ensure the perpetual=20
teaching of EMT.

     All this said, a warning is appropriate.  Arbitrage has=20
looked easy recently.  But this is not a form of investing that=20
guarantees profits of 20% a year or, for that matter, profits of=20
any kind.  As noted, the market is reasonably efficient much of=20
the time: For every arbitrage opportunity we seized in that 63-
year period, many more were foregone because they seemed=20
properly-priced.

     An investor cannot obtain superior profits from stocks by=20
simply committing to a specific investment category or style.  He=20
can earn them only by carefully evaluating facts and continuously=20
exercising discipline.  Investing in arbitrage situations, per=20
se, is no better a strategy than selecting a portfolio by=20
throwing darts.

<b>New York Stock Exchange Listing</b>

     Berkshire=92s shares were listed on the New York Stock=20
Exchange on November 29, 1988.  On pages 50-51 we reproduce the=20
letter we sent to shareholders concerning the listing.

     Let me clarify one point not dealt with in the letter:=20
Though our round lot for trading on the NYSE is ten shares, any=20
number of shares from one on up can be bought or sold.

     As the letter explains, our primary goal in listing was to=20
reduce transaction costs, and we believe this goal is being=20
achieved.  Generally, the spread between the bid and asked price=20
on the NYSE has been well below the spread that prevailed in the=20
over-the-counter market.

     Henderson Brothers, Inc., the specialist in our shares, is=20
the oldest continuing specialist firm on the Exchange; its=20
progenitor, William Thomas Henderson, bought his seat for $500 on=20
September 8, 1861. (Recently, seats were selling for about=20
$625,000.) Among the 54 firms acting as specialists, HBI ranks=20
second in number of stocks assigned, with 83.  We were pleased=20
when Berkshire was allocated to HBI, and have been delighted with=20
the firm=92s performance.  Jim Maguire, Chairman of HBI, personally=20
manages the trading in Berkshire, and we could not be in better=20
hands.

     In two respects our goals probably differ somewhat from=20
those of most listed companies.  First, we do not want to=20
maximize the price at which Berkshire shares trade.  We wish=20
instead for them to trade in a narrow range centered at intrinsic=20
business value (which we hope increases at a reasonable - or,=20
better yet, unreasonable - rate).  Charlie and I are bothered as=20
much by significant overvaluation as significant undervaluation. =20
Both extremes will inevitably produce results for many=20
shareholders that will differ sharply from Berkshire=92s business=20
results.  If our stock price instead consistently mirrors=20
business value, each of our shareholders will receive an=20
investment result that roughly parallels the business results of=20
Berkshire during his holding period.

     Second, we wish for very little trading activity.  If we ran=20
a private business with a few passive partners, we would be=20
disappointed if those partners, and their replacements,=20
frequently wanted to leave the partnership.  Running a public=20
company, we feel the same way.

     Our goal is to attract long-term owners who, at the time of=20
purchase, have no timetable or price target for sale but plan=20
instead to stay with us indefinitely.  We don=92t understand the=20
CEO who wants lots of stock activity, for that can be achieved=20
only if many of his owners are constantly exiting.  At what other=20
organization - school, club, church, etc. - do leaders cheer when=20
members leave? (However, if there were a broker whose livelihood=20
depended upon the membership turnover in such organizations, you=20
could be sure that there would be at least one proponent of=20
activity, as in: =93There hasn=92t been much going on in Christianity=20
for a while; maybe we should switch to Buddhism next week.=93)

     Of course, some Berkshire owners will need or want to sell=20
from time to time, and we wish for good replacements who will pay=20
them a fair price.  Therefore we try, through our policies,=20
performance, and communications, to attract new shareholders who=20
understand our operations, share our time horizons, and measure=20
us as we measure ourselves.  If we can continue to attract this=20
sort of shareholder - and, just as important, can continue to be=20
uninteresting to those with short-term or unrealistic=20
expectations - Berkshire shares should consistently sell at=20
prices reasonably related to business value.

<b>David L. Dodd</b>

     Dave Dodd, my friend and teacher for 38 years, died last=20
year at age 93.  Most of you don=92t know of him.  Yet any long-
time shareholder of Berkshire is appreciably wealthier because of=20
the indirect influence he had upon our company.

     Dave spent a lifetime teaching at Columbia University, and=20
he co-authored <i>Security Analysis</i> with Ben Graham.  From the=20
moment I arrived at Columbia, Dave personally encouraged and=20
educated me; one influence was as important as the other. =20
Everything he taught me, directly or through his book, made=20
sense.  Later, through dozens of letters, he continued my=20
education right up until his death.

     I have known many professors of finance and investments but=20
I have never seen any, except for Ben Graham, who was the match=20
of Dave.  The proof of his talent is the record of his students:=20
No other teacher of investments has sent forth so many who have=20
achieved unusual success.

     When students left Dave=92s classroom, they were equipped to=20
invest intelligently for a lifetime because the principles he=20
taught were simple, sound, useful, and enduring.  Though these=20
may appear to be unremarkable virtues, the teaching of principles=20
embodying them has been rare.

     It=92s particularly impressive that Dave could practice as=20
well as preach. just as Keynes became wealthy by applying his=20
academic ideas to a very small purse, so, too, did Dave.  Indeed,=20
his financial performance far outshone that of Keynes, who began=20
as a market-timer (leaning on business and credit-cycle theory)=20
and converted, after much thought, to value investing.  Dave was=20
right from the start.

     In Berkshire=92s investments, Charlie and I have employed the=20
principles taught by Dave and Ben Graham.  Our prosperity is the=20
fruit of their intellectual tree.

<b>Miscellaneous</b>

     We hope to buy more businesses that are similar to the ones=20
we have, and we can use some help.  If you have a business that=20
fits the following criteria, call me or, preferably, write.

     Here=92s what we=92re looking for:

     (1) large purchases (at least $10 million of after-tax=20
         earnings),

     (2) demonstrated consistent earning power (future projections=20
         are of little interest to us, nor are =93turnaround=94=20
         situations),

     (3) businesses earning good returns on equity while employing=20
         little or no debt,

     (4) management in place (we can=92t supply it),

     (5) simple businesses (if there=92s lots of technology, we won=92t=20
         understand it),

     (6) an offering price (we don=92t want to waste our time or that=20
         of the seller by talking, even preliminarily, about a=20
         transaction when price is unknown).

     We will not engage in unfriendly takeovers.  We can promise=20
complete confidentiality and a very fast answer - customarily=20
within five minutes - as to whether we=92re interested.  We prefer=20
to buy for cash, but will consider issuing stock when we receive=20
as much in intrinsic business value as we give.

     Our favorite form of purchase is one fitting the Blumkin-
Friedman-Heldman mold.  In cases like these, the company=92s owner-
managers wish to generate significant amounts of cash, sometimes=20
for themselves, but often for their families or inactive=20
shareholders.  However, these managers also wish to remain=20
significant owners who continue to run their companies just as=20
they have in the past.  We think we offer a particularly good fit=20
for owners with these objectives and invite potential sellers to=20
check us out by contacting people with whom we have done business=20
in the past.

     Charlie and I frequently get approached about acquisitions=20
that don=92t come close to meeting our tests: We=92ve found that if=20
you advertise an interest in buying collies, a lot of people will=20
call hoping to sell you their cocker spaniels.  Our interest in=20
new ventures, turnarounds, or auction-like sales can best be=20
expressed by another Goldwynism: =93Please include me out.=94

     Besides being interested in the purchase of businesses as=20
described above, we are also interested in the negotiated=20
purchase of large, but not controlling, blocks of stock=20
comparable to those we hold in Cap Cities and Salomon.  We have a=20
special interest in purchasing convertible preferreds as a long-
term investment, as we did at Salomon.

